-- An `Iter` combinator must report a callee that is not a function, and
-- report it whether or not the source has any elements to call it on.
--
-- The check used to sit inside the per-element loop, so an empty source
-- skipped it and the combinator quietly answered `{}` — the one input where
-- a program could pass a number as its callback and hear nothing about it.
-- It is made once per combinator now, which is both cheaper and the reason
-- this case reports at all.
class Main
  static fn main()
    local empty: table<integer> = {}
    local callback: any = 42
    local out: any = Iter.map(empty, callback)
    println(out)
  end
end
